{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel were fruitful.</b> They bore fruit like a tree yields its fruit." "<b>And swarmed.</b> Like \"let the waters swarm\" (Genesis 1:20). Only the first [verb, ויפרו] is of the intransitive verbs [פעלים עומדים], like הלך, ישב, עמד, שכב, and the second is of the transitives [יוצאים], dependent on something else, like אכל, שמר, שכר — \"he ate the bread,\" \"he kept the matter,\" \"he hired the donkey\"; these are called פועל יוצא. Now \"let the waters swarm\" is of the transitives, but this second one, וישרצו, is of the intransitives. And this is the paradigm, like the paradigm of שב: once intransitive, like \"and He will turn and gather you\" (Deuteronomy 30:3), and once transitive, like \"and YHWH your Elohim will turn your captivity\" (ibid.). Perhaps the word וישרצו hints that their wives bore twins and more, and I myself have seen four sons born to one woman, and the physicians give a reason up to seven that can arrive in one womb." "<b>And multiplied.</b> That they did not die in the manner of a numerous people." "<b>And grew strong.</b> That they were possessed of strong bones, and the phrase במאד מאד [exceedingly], that they could not be more. And the bet of במאד מאד is like the bet of \"and they set out at the first\" (Numbers 10:13), for it is written \"first they shall set out\" (Numbers 2:9)." "<b>And the land was filled.</b> It is the land of Goshen." "Raamses, with a sheva naḥ under the ayin, for the first [sheva], which is under the ayin and joined to the resh, is naḥ, and the second, under the mem, is na`, for it is like the onset of na` ha-safa."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:43.062607301Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}